(a) Violation of this article shall be punishable as a misdemeanor local ordinance violation and shall include one or more of the following penalties:
(1) A monetary fine up to a maximum of $1,000.00;
(2) Maximum imprisonment of 60 days;
(3) Suspension or revocation of the registrant's arcade license and any other city licenses held by the registrant, including, but not limited to, license for the sale of alcoholic beverages;
(4) Revocation of machine registration and prohibition of machine operation at any location which does not require an arcade license.
(b) In addition, the city or designee may notify the appropriate state agencies of any violations pertaining to the improper use of bona fide coin-operated amusement machines. The agencies to be notified may include, but not be limited to, the state revenue department, the state bureau of investigation, and the Georgia Lottery Corporation, and violators may be prosecuted under O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35, and any other applicable provision of state or federal law.
(Code 1995, § 10-33; Ord. of 1-9-2001)

335:
This chapter is adopted for the following purposes, among others:
(1) To ensure equitable handling of all subdivision plats by providing uniform procedures and standards for the subdivider;
(2) To ensure, in general, the wise development of new land areas, in harmony with the comprehensive plan of the city;
(3) To ensure the accurate description of property, the identification of property boundaries with monuments, and the proper recording of property descriptions in public records;
(4) To help eliminate the costly maintenance problems which occur when streets and lots are laid out without proper consideration given to various public purposes;
(5) To protect lot purchasers who generally lack the specialized knowledge to evaluate subdivision improvements and design;
(6) To implement the Georgia Plat and Condominium Plan Recording Act of 2017, as amended, and other applicable state laws;
(7) To ensure equitable handling of all land disturbance permit applications and development permit applications by establishing uniform procedures and standards for land disturbance and development permitting;
(8) To establish standards and specifications for improvements to be constructed, including facilities to be dedicated to the city; and
(9) To establish procedures and requirements for buildings and provide for building inspections and plan reviews.
(Ord. of 4-9-2024, att.(103))

341:
The following types of land subdivisions, transfers, and sales are specifically exempted from the plat approval requirements of this chapter; provided, however, that such exemptions shall not apply to land development requirements and improvement requirements of this chapter or compliance with other applicable requirements of this chapter:
(1) Retracement surveys and easement surveys, as specifically authorized in this chapter;
(2) The creation and sale of cemetery plots;
(3) The sale of lots consistent with previously approved and recorded plats or deeds;
(4) The creation of leaseholds for space within a multiple occupancy building or the division of property into leaseholds (but not for sale) for commercial, industrial, or institutional use;
(5) The creation of leaseholds (but not for sale) for the agricultural use of property where the use does not involve the construction of a building to be used as a residence or for other purposes not directly related to agricultural use of the land or crops or livestock raised thereon.
(6) Any division of land to heirs through a judicial estate proceeding, or any division of land pursuant to a judicial partition, or any division of land occurring from the foreclosure of a deed of trust; provided, however, that such exemption shall not be construed as obligating the city to issue permits on such land divisions if the resulting lots or parcels fail to meet any applicable zoning provisions (see the city zoning ordinance) regarding lot size, lot width, or other dimensional requirements.
(Ord. of 4-9-2024, att.(109))

